    id: "am2c-012",
    topicRef: "am2c",
    topicTitle: "Optimal error bounds 12",
    difficulty: "Standard",
    answerType: "expression",
    questionText:
      "Show that \\( x^{-1/2}e^{-1/x} = o(x^{M}) \\) as \\( x \\to 0^{+} \\) for every fixed positive integer \\( M \\), i.e. the optimal error is beyond all algebraic orders (exponentially accurate).",
    marks: 3,
    examStyle: true,
    yearCreated: 2026,
    tags: ["exponential accuracy", "beyond all orders", "little-o"],
    workedSolution: {
      steps: [
        { stepNumber: 1, description: "Recall what little-o requires.", workingLatex: "g(x) = o(x^{M}) \\iff \\frac{g(x)}{x^{M}} \\to 0 \\quad (x\\to0^{+}).", explanation: "The statement \\(g = o(x^M)\\) means \\(g\\) is negligible compared with \\(x^M\\): the ratio \\(g/x^M\\) tends to zero. This is strictly stronger than \\(g\\to0\\); it says \\(g\\) decays faster than that particular power. We must verify it for every \\(M\\), not just one." },
        { stepNumber: 2, description: "Form the ratio to be shown to vanish.", workingLatex: "\\frac{x^{-1/2}e^{-1/x}}{x^{M}} = x^{-(M+1/2)}\\,e^{-1/x}.", explanation: "Dividing by \\(x^M\\) and combining the powers (\\(-1/2 - M\\)) gives \\(x^{-(M+1/2)}e^{-1/x}\\). The power is now negative with a large exponent, so as \\(x\\to0^{+}\\) the prefactor \\(x^{-(M+1/2)}\\) blows up while \\(e^{-1/x}\\) collapses. The whole point is that the exponential wins." },
        { stepNumber: 3, description: "Substitute \\( u = 1/x \\to \\infty \\).", workingLatex: "x^{-(M+1/2)}e^{-1/x} = u^{\\,M+1/2}\\,e^{-u}, \\qquad u = \\frac{1}{x} \\to \\infty.", explanation: "As \\(x\\to0^{+}\\) the new variable \\(u = 1/x\\) tends to \\(+\\infty\\). Since \\(x^{-(M+1/2)} = u^{M+1/2}\\), the awkward small-\\(x\\) limit becomes a clean large-\\(u\\) limit. This recasts the problem as the standard exponential-versus-power race." },
        { stepNumber: 4, description: "Justify exponential-beats-power from the series.", workingLatex: "e^{u} = \\sum_{k\\ge0}\\frac{u^k}{k!} > \\frac{u^{\\,p+1}}{(p+1)!} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; u^{\\,p}e^{-u} < \\frac{(p+1)!}{u} \\quad (p = M+\\tfrac12).", explanation: "To see why the exponential wins, keep just one term of the series for \\(e^{u}\\): picking the \\((\\lceil p\\rceil+1)\\)-th power gives \\(e^{u} > u^{p+1}/(p+1)!\\) for \\(u>0\\). Rearranging shows \\(u^{p}e^{-u} < (p+1)!/u\\), an explicit bound that makes the decay concrete rather than asserted. This is cleaner than L'Hopital because it needs no repeated differentiation and exposes the \\(1/u\\) rate directly." },
        { stepNumber: 5, description: "Take the limit.", workingLatex: "0 < u^{\\,M+1/2}\\,e^{-u} < \\frac{(M+3/2)!}{u} \\xrightarrow[u\\to\\infty]{} 0.", explanation: "The bound \\((p+1)!/u\\) tends to \\(0\\) as \\(u\\to\\infty\\), so by the squeeze the product \\(u^{M+1/2}e^{-u}\\to0\\). Crucially this holds for every finite \\(M\\), since the constant \\((M+3/2)!\\) is fixed once \\(M\\) is chosen — that universal quantifier is exactly what \"beyond all orders\" requires. No power of \\(u\\), however large its exponent, can hold the exponential off." },
        { stepNumber: 6, description: "Conclude exponential accuracy.", workingLatex: "\\Rightarrow\\; x^{-1/2}e^{-1/x} = o(x^{M}) \\quad\\forall\\,M \\in \\mathbb{Z}^{+}.", explanation: "Since the ratio vanishes for every \\(M\\), the optimal error is smaller than every power of \\(x\\): genuine exponential accuracy, invisible to the algebraic series term-by-term. Note this does \\emph{not} say the error is zero — it is strictly positive, just beyond all algebraic orders, so confusing \\(o(x^M)\\)-for-all-\\(M\\) with \\(=0\\) is a real conceptual error. Capturing that exponentially small remainder requires exponential asymptotics — Borel summation, or resurgence and the Stokes phenomenon — which look past the power series entirely." },
      ],
      finalAnswer: "\\( x^{-1/2}e^{-1/x}/x^{M} = u^{M+1/2}e^{-u} \\to 0 \\); hence \\( o(x^{M}) \\) for all \\( M \\).",
    },
