      steps: [
        { stepNumber: 1, description: "List the terms \\( (-1)^n (n+1)!\\,(0.1)^n \\).", workingLatex: "1,\\,-0.2,\\,0.06,\\,-0.024,\\,0.012,\\,-0.0072,\\,0.00504,\\,-0.0040320,\\,0.00362880.", explanation: "These are the terms for \\(n = 0\\) to \\(n = 8\\); for instance \\(n = 1\\) gives \\(-2!\\cdot0.1 = -0.2\\) and \\(n = 8\\) gives \\(9!\\cdot(0.1)^8 = 0.0036288\\). The magnitudes \\((n+1)!\\,(0.1)^n\\) decrease to a minimum near \\(n = 8\\), confirming the stated truncation point. Their alternating signs will control the error." },
        { stepNumber: 2, description: "Accumulate the running total.", workingLatex: "1 - 0.2 = 0.8,\\;\\; +0.06 = 0.86,\\;\\; -0.024 = 0.836,\\;\\; +0.012 = 0.848,\\;\\dots", explanation: "Adding left to right, the partial totals oscillate around the eventual value, the signature of an alternating series. Pairing each positive with the following negative keeps the running total steady and limits rounding error. The swings shrink as we proceed, confirming we are still inside the decreasing run where the enclosure bound is valid." },
        { stepNumber: 3, description: "Read off the optimal partial sum.", workingLatex: "S_8 = \\sum_{n=0}^{8}(-1)^n (n+1)!\\,(0.1)^n = 0.8454368\\ldots", explanation: "Completing the nine-term sum gives \\(S_8 = 0.8454368\\), the optimal-truncation estimate of \\(G(0.1)\\). This is the best the series can offer at \\(x = 0.1\\); the next term would be the growing tail. We compare it against the exact value next." },
        { stepNumber: 4, description: "Compute the realised error.", workingLatex: "\\bigl| G(0.1) - S_8 \\bigr| = |0.8436666 - 0.8454368| \\approx 1.8\\times 10^{-3}.", explanation: "Subtract the exact value from the partial sum to get the actual error, about \\(1.8\\times10^{-3}\\) to one significant figure. Note this is roughly ten times larger than the \\(F\\)-series error \\(1.77\\times10^{-4}\\) at the same \\(x\\), because \\(G\\)'s coefficients grow one factorial faster and so its least term is bigger. Faster-growing coefficients mean a less accurate optimal truncation, even though both series are exponentially accurate in the limit." },
        { stepNumber: 5, description: "Compare with the least-term bound.", workingLatex: "1.8\\times10^{-3} < |a_9| = (9{+}1)!\\,(0.1)^9 = 10!\\,(0.1)^9 = 3.6\\times10^{-3}.", explanation: "The first omitted term is the \\(n = 9\\) term, whose coefficient is \\((9+1)! = 10!\\); evaluating gives \\(3.6\\times10^{-3}\\). The realised error lies safely within this least-term bound, as the alternating-series enclosure guarantees, and again sits at about half the bound. A common slip is to use \\(9!\\) instead of \\(10!\\) for the \\(n = 9\\) coefficient here — the coefficient is \\((n+1)!\\), not \\(n!\\), so dropping the \\(+1\\) would understate the bound by a factor of \\(10\\)." },
      ],
      finalAnswer: "\\( S_8 = 0.845437\\ldots \\); error \\( \\approx 1.8\\times10^{-3} \\) (within the least-term bound).",
      canonicalAnswer: "0.8454368",
