    tags: ["least term", "Stirling", "exponential accuracy"],
    workedSolution: {
      steps: [
        { stepNumber: 1, description: "Form the ratio of magnitudes.", workingLatex: "\\frac{|a_{n+1}|}{|a_n|} = \\frac{(n+1)!\\,z^{-(n+1)}}{n!\\,z^{-n}}.", explanation: "Here the small parameter is \\(1/z\\) and we take \\(z\\to\\infty\\), so the series runs in inverse powers of \\(z\\). The ratio test still finds where the terms stop shrinking. We simplify the factorial and power pieces next, watching the negative exponents." },
        { stepNumber: 2, description: "Cancel the factorials and powers.", workingLatex: "\\frac{(n+1)!}{n!} = n+1, \\qquad \\frac{z^{-(n+1)}}{z^{-n}} = z^{-(n+1)+n} = z^{-1}.", explanation: "The factorials give \\((n+1)!/n! = n+1\\), and subtracting the negative exponents gives \\(-(n+1)-(-n) = -1\\), so the powers leave \\(z^{-1}\\). Mind the double negative — a dropped sign would put \\(z\\) on top and reverse the conclusion about which way the terms move. We combine these next." },
        { stepNumber: 3, description: "Write the simplified ratio.", workingLatex: "\\frac{|a_{n+1}|}{|a_n|} = (n+1)\\,z^{-1} = \\frac{n+1}{z}.", explanation: "Multiplying gives the ratio \\((n+1)/z\\). Terms decrease while \\(n+1 < z\\) and grow once \\(n+1 > z\\); the large \\(z\\) lets many terms shrink before the factorial takes over. This is the mirror image of the \\(x\\to0\\) case, where the small \\(x\\) let only \\(\\sim1/x\\) terms shrink." },
        { stepNumber: 4, description: "Find the least-term index.", workingLatex: "\\frac{n+1}{z} = 1 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; n+1 = z \\;\\Rightarrow\\; n^{*} \\approx z.", explanation: "The smallest term sits at an index of order \\(z\\) — contrast the \\(x\\to0\\) case where it was \\(1/x\\). For large \\(z\\) this is a large index, so optimal truncation here keeps many terms. The minimum scales with \\(z\\) because the genuinely small parameter is \\(1/z\\); expecting it near \\(1/z\\) (essentially zero terms) is the classic large-argument trap." },
        { stepNumber: 5, description: "Write the least-term magnitude.", workingLatex: "a_{n^{*}} = z!\\,z^{-z}.", explanation: "Substitute \\(n^{*} = z\\) into \\(n!\\,z^{-n}\\): the factorial argument is \\(z\\) and the power is \\(z^{-z}\\). Estimating \\(z!\\) via Stirling is all that remains. As in every case in this topic, the factorial and the power will nearly cancel, leaving only an exponential." },
        { stepNumber: 6, description: "Substitute Stirling for the factorial.", workingLatex: "z!\\,z^{-z} \\sim \\sqrt{2\\pi z}\\left(\\frac{z}{e}\\right)^{z} z^{-z} = \\sqrt{2\\pi z}\\;\\frac{z^{z}}{e^{z}}\\,z^{-z}.", explanation: "Stirling gives \\(z! \\sim \\sqrt{2\\pi z}(z/e)^z\\), and expanding the bracket as \\((z/e)^z = z^z/e^z\\) lays the \\(z^z\\) bare next to the term's \\(z^{-z}\\). Keeping the prefactor \\(\\sqrt{2\\pi z}\\) is essential — it becomes the algebraic order. The two opposing powers of \\(z\\) are now ready to cancel." },
        { stepNumber: 7, description: "Cancel the \\( z^{\\pm z} \\) factors.", workingLatex: "z^{z}\\cdot z^{-z} = z^{0} = 1 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; z!\\,z^{-z} \\sim \\sqrt{2\\pi z}\\;e^{-z}.", explanation: "Adding the exponents \\(z + (-z) = 0\\) annihilates the \\(z^{z}\\) against \\(z^{-z}\\), leaving \\(\\sqrt{2\\pi z}\\,e^{-z}\\). This is the same cancellation mechanism as the \\(x\\to0\\) case, just with \\(z\\) large rather than \\(1/x\\) large. Only the exponential and the prefactor survive." },
        { stepNumber: 8, description: "State the order.", workingLatex: "a_{n^{*}} = \\sqrt{2\\pi}\\,z^{1/2}\\,e^{-z} = O\\!\\left(z^{1/2}e^{-z}\\right).", explanation: "Writing \\(\\sqrt{2\\pi z} = \\sqrt{2\\pi}\\,z^{1/2}\\) gives the order. Note the prefactor power is \\(z^{+1/2}\\) here, not \\(z^{-1/2}\\), because Stirling's prefactor \\(\\sqrt{2\\pi z}\\) grows with the large argument \\(z\\) — a sign flip from the \\(x^{-1/2}\\) of the small-parameter case, where the argument was \\(1/x\\). Either way \\(e^{-z}\\) dominates the mild power, so the optimal error is exponentially small in \\(z\\) and \\(o(z^{-M})\\) for every \\(M\\)." },
      ],
      finalAnswer: "\\( n^{*} \\approx z \\); optimal error \\( a_{n^{*}} \\sim \\sqrt{2\\pi z}\\,e^{-z} = O\\!\\left(z^{1/2}e^{-z}\\right) \\).",
      canonicalAnswer: "sqrt(2*pi*z)*exp(-z)",
    },
