    id: "am2c-021",
    topicRef: "am2c",
    topicTitle: "Optimal error bounds 21",
    difficulty: "Challenge",
    answerType: "expression",
    questionText:
      "Using integration by parts, derive the asymptotic expansion \\( E_1(x) = \\int_x^{\\infty}\\frac{e^{-t}}{t}\\,dt \\sim e^{-x}\\left(\\frac{1}{x} - \\frac{1}{x^2} + \\frac{2!}{x^3} - \\cdots\\right) \\) as \\( x \\to \\infty \\), and show the remainder after \\( N \\) terms is \\( O\\!\\left(e^{-x}x^{-(N+1)}\\right) \\). Hence give the optimal-truncation error order.",
    marks: 6,
    examStyle: true,
    yearCreated: 2026,
    tags: ["exponential integral", "integration by parts", "optimal truncation", "error bound"],
    workedSolution: {
      steps: [
        { stepNumber: 1, description: "Choose parts for the first integration.", workingLatex: "u = \\frac1t,\\; dv = e^{-t}dt \\;\\Rightarrow\\; du = -\\frac{1}{t^2}dt,\\; v = -e^{-t}.", explanation: "Integration by parts will trade the integrand for one with a higher inverse power of \\(t\\), which is smaller for large \\(t\\). We differentiate the algebraic factor \\(1/t\\) (it gets simpler) and integrate the exponential (it is invariant up to sign). This is the natural choice that generates the asymptotic series." },
        { stepNumber: 2, description: "Integrate by parts once.", workingLatex: "E_1(x) = \\Bigl[-\\frac{e^{-t}}{t}\\Bigr]_x^{\\infty} - \\int_x^{\\infty}\\frac{e^{-t}}{t^2}\\,dt = \\frac{e^{-x}}{x} - \\int_x^{\\infty}\\frac{e^{-t}}{t^2}\\,dt.", explanation: "The boundary term \\([uv]\\) vanishes at \\(t = \\infty\\) (exponential decay) and gives \\(+e^{-x}/x\\) at the lower limit \\(t = x\\). The new integral has \\(t^{-2}\\), one power smaller than before. This is the first term of the series plus a remainder integral." },
        { stepNumber: 3, description: "Integrate by parts again.", workingLatex: "\\int_x^{\\infty}\\frac{e^{-t}}{t^2}\\,dt = \\frac{e^{-x}}{x^2} - 2\\int_x^{\\infty}\\frac{e^{-t}}{t^3}\\,dt.", explanation: "Repeat with \\(u = 1/t^2\\), whose derivative is \\(-2/t^3\\); the constant \\(2\\) appears and the inverse power climbs to \\(t^{-3}\\). Each pass pulls out one more term of the series and one more factorial factor. The pattern is now visible." },
        { stepNumber: 4, description: "Iterate to \\( N \\) terms.", workingLatex: "E_1(x) = e^{-x}\\sum_{n=0}^{N}\\frac{(-1)^n n!}{x^{n+1}} + R_N, \\quad R_N = (-1)^{N+1}(N+1)!\\!\\int_x^{\\infty}\\frac{e^{-t}}{t^{N+2}}\\,dt.", explanation: "Continuing the same step \\(N\\) times produces the alternating factorial series with terms \\((-1)^n n!\\,x^{-(n+1)}\\), all carrying the common factor \\(e^{-x}\\). The leftover is an exact remainder integral whose prefactor is the next factorial \\((N+1)!\\). Keeping \\(R_N\\) exact lets us bound it rigorously rather than guess." },
        { stepNumber: 5, description: "Bound the remainder integral.", workingLatex: "|R_N| = (N+1)!\\int_x^{\\infty}\\frac{e^{-t}}{t^{N+2}}\\,dt \\le \\frac{(N+1)!}{x^{N+2}}\\int_x^{\\infty}e^{-t}\\,dt.", explanation: "On the range \\([x,\\infty)\\) the factor \\(t^{-(N+2)}\\) is largest at the left endpoint \\(t = x\\), so replacing it by the constant \\(x^{-(N+2)}\\) can only increase the integrand. Pulling that constant out leaves \\(\\int_x^\\infty e^{-t}dt\\), an elementary integral. This is the standard way to bound a decaying-weight integral." },
        { stepNumber: 6, description: "Evaluate the elementary integral.", workingLatex: "\\int_x^{\\infty}e^{-t}\\,dt = e^{-x} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; |R_N| \\le \\frac{(N+1)!\\,e^{-x}}{x^{N+2}}.", explanation: "The remaining integral is just \\(e^{-x}\\). Substituting back gives the rigorous bound \\((N+1)!\\,e^{-x}/x^{N+2}\\) on the remainder. This is comparable to the first omitted term \\(e^{-x}(N+1)!\\,x^{-(N+2)}\\), so the bound is sharp up to the factorial constant." },
        { stepNumber: 7, description: "State the order of the remainder.", workingLatex: "R_N = O\\!\\left(e^{-x}\\,x^{-(N+1)}\\right).", explanation: "Up to constants the remainder behaves like \\(e^{-x}x^{-(N+1)}\\), matching the order of the first omitted term. So truncating after \\(N\\) terms incurs an error of this size. We now minimise it over \\(N\\)." },
        { stepNumber: 8, description: "Locate the optimal truncation.", workingLatex: "\\frac{|R_{N+1}|}{|R_N|} \\sim \\frac{N+1}{x} = 1 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; N \\approx x.", explanation: "The first-omitted-term bound is smallest where the ratio of consecutive bounds passes through \\(1\\), i.e. at \\(N \\approx x\\). As in the other inverse-power series, the optimal \\(N\\) tracks \\(x\\). We substitute this into the least term next." },
        { stepNumber: 9, description: "Write the optimal least term.", workingLatex: "|R_N|_{\\text{opt}} \\sim e^{-x}\\,x!\\,x^{-(x+1)}.", explanation: "At \\(N \\approx x\\) the least term is \\(e^{-x}(N+1)!\\,x^{-(N+2)}\\) evaluated near \\(N = x\\), i.e. \\(e^{-x}\\,x!\\,x^{-(x+1)}\\). Estimating \\(x!\\) by Stirling will collapse the powers. Note the extra \\(e^{-x}\\) prefactor that the whole series carries." },
        { stepNumber: 10, description: "Substitute Stirling for \\( x! \\).", workingLatex: "|R_N|_{\\text{opt}} \\sim e^{-x}\\,\\sqrt{2\\pi x}\\left(\\frac{x}{e}\\right)^{x} x^{-(x+1)} = e^{-x}\\,\\sqrt{2\\pi x}\\;\\frac{x^{x}}{e^{x}}\\,x^{-(x+1)}.", explanation: "Stirling gives \\(x! \\sim \\sqrt{2\\pi x}\\,(x/e)^x = \\sqrt{2\\pi x}\\,x^x e^{-x}\\); insert it and expand the bracket. Two distinct exponentials are now present — the series prefactor \\(e^{-x}\\) and Stirling's own \\(e^{-x}\\) — plus the opposing powers \\(x^{x}\\) and \\(x^{-(x+1)}\\). We handle the powers and the exponentials in turn." },
        { stepNumber: 11, description: "Cancel the large powers of \\( x \\).", workingLatex: "x^{x}\\cdot x^{-(x+1)} = x^{x-(x+1)} = x^{-1} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\sqrt{2\\pi x}\\,x^{-1} = \\sqrt{2\\pi}\\,x^{-1/2}.", explanation: "Adding exponents, \\(x^{x}\\) reduces \\(x^{-(x+1)}\\) to just \\(x^{-1}\\), the usual near-total cancellation of the factorial against the power. Then \\(\\sqrt{2\\pi x}\\,x^{-1} = \\sqrt{2\\pi}\\,x^{1/2-1} = \\sqrt{2\\pi}\\,x^{-1/2}\\), giving the algebraic prefactor. Only the two exponentials are left to combine." },
        { stepNumber: 12, description: "Combine the two exponentials.", workingLatex: "e^{-x}\\cdot e^{-x} = e^{-2x} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; |R_N|_{\\text{opt}} \\sim \\sqrt{2\\pi}\\,x^{-1/2}e^{-2x} = O\\!\\left(x^{-1/2}e^{-2x}\\right).", explanation: "The two factors of \\(e^{-x}\\) — one from the series prefactor, one from Stirling — multiply to \\(e^{-2x}\\). That doubled exponential is the distinctive feature of \\(E_1\\)'s optimal accuracy: it is sharper than the bare \\(e^{-x}\\) prefactor of a single term, because at optimal truncation the least term itself is already exponentially small in \\(x\\). The result is \\(O(x^{-1/2}e^{-2x})\\), again beyond all algebraic orders." },
      ],
      finalAnswer: "\\( R_N = O\\!\\left(e^{-x}x^{-(N+1)}\\right) \\); at \\( N\\approx x \\), optimal error \\( = O\\!\\left(x^{-1/2}e^{-2x}\\right) \\).",
    },
